  <graph edgedefault="undirected">
    <node id="Ständerat">
      <data key="d0">Ständerat</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ständerat is the upper house of the Swiss Federal Assembly, with representatives elected from the cantons, serving distinct legislative functions alongside the Nationalrat (National Council).&lt;SEP&gt;The Council of States, the second chamber of the Federal Assembly, representing the cantons in the Swiss legislative process with two members per canton.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ständerätin">
      <data key="d0">Ständerätin</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Ständerätin is a female member of the Ständerat, serving to represent the interests of her canton in the Swiss legislative process.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-001</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kanton">
      <data key="d0">Kanton</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The term Kanton refers to the administrative divisions of Switzerland, with each representing a region that elects representatives to the Ständerat.&lt;SEP&gt;Kantons are administrative divisions in Switzerland, each possessing unique legal and judicial systems, such as different levels of courts and judicial authorities. They have political autonomy and can regulate certain aspects independently.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nationalrat">
      <data key="d0">Nationalrat</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Nationalrat is the lower house of the Swiss Federal Assembly, responsible for discussing and voting on legislative proposals, working alongside the Ständerat.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Council, a chamber of the Federal Assembly, composed of representatives elected to represent the Swiss population according to their demographic distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vereinigte Bundesversammlung">
      <data key="d0">Vereinigte Bundesversammlung</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Vereinigte Bundesversammlung is a joint assembly of the Nationalrat and the Ständerat, convened for specific legislative purposes, such as elections of federal officials.&lt;SEP&gt;The joint assembly of the two houses of the Federal Assembly of Switzerland, responsible for electing federal judges.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-003</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bundesrat">
      <data key="d0">Bundesrat</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Bundesrat is the federal executive authority of Switzerland, consisting of seven members, each presiding over a department and collectively responsible for the government.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bundesrat is the collective executive body of the Swiss Federal Council, composed of members who oversee various departments and functions within the Swiss government. It does not function as a head of state but is considered the collective head of state of Switzerland.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-002</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bundespräsident">
      <data key="d0">Bundespräsident</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Bundespräsident is the president of the Swiss Federal Council, elected annually from among its members to lead the government temporarily but does not serve as a head of state.&lt;SEP&gt;The President of the Swiss Confederation, serving as Primus inter Pares within the Federal Council, representing the council but not acting as a head of state.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bundespräsident is a member of the Bundesrat elected to serve as the President of the Swiss Federal Council for a one-year term, representing Switzerland at ceremonial functions but not holding the powers of a head of state.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-002</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Landsgemeinde">
      <data key="d0">Landsgemeinde</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Landsgemeinde is a traditional assembly held annually in certain Swiss cantons, where citizens directly participate in legislative decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-001</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kantonsvertretung">
      <data key="d0">Kantonsvertretung</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Kantonsvertretung refers to the representation concept within the Swiss political system, indicating how cantonal interests are represented, though often deemed misleading in the context of the Ständerat.&lt;SEP&gt;The concept of representation of the cantons in the national parliament, exemplified by the Council of States.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Instruktionsverbot">
      <data key="d0">Instruktionsverbot</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Instruktionsverbot is the principle that members of the Ständerat and Nationalrat are not bound by instructions from cantons or political parties during the execution of their mandates, allowing independence in their legislative processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-001</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Schweizerische Eidgenossenschaft">
      <data key="d0">Schweizerische Eidgenossenschaft</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Swiss Confederation, operating with a unique system that combines elements of both parliamentary and presidential democracy, known for its directorial system and attributes that distinguish it from other modern democratic models.&lt;SEP&gt;The Schweizerische Eidgenossenschaft, known as the Swiss Confederation, is the federal state of Switzerland that governs the country along with its cantonal entities.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-002</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Föderalismus">
      <data key="d0">Föderalismus</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Federalism in Switzerland, characterized by significant autonomy among the cantons, allowing them to grant their municipalities considerable leeway and involvement in political decision-making.&lt;SEP&gt;Föderalismus refers to the political system in Switzerland that divides authority between the federal government and cantonal governments, emphasizing autonomy and collaboration.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Direkte Demokratie">
      <data key="d0">Direkte Demokratie</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Direct democracy in Switzerland, enabling citizens to influence government actions through initiatives and referendums, impacting government activity at various levels.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Volksrechte">
      <data key="d0">Volksrechte</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The rights of citizens in Switzerland, rooted in both assembly and ballot democracy, performing functions that include control, initiation, and legitimization of political processes.&lt;SEP&gt;The Volksrechte, or popular rights, represent the rights of citizens to participate in decision-making processes in Switzerland, including various forms of democratic participation such as voting and referendums.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gewaltenteilung">
      <data key="d0">Gewaltenteilung</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The principle of separation of powers in Switzerland, ensuring that the government cannot be dismissed between elections, and the parliament cannot be dissolved by the government.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bundesversammlung">
      <data key="d0">Bundesversammlung</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Federal Assembly of Switzerland, consisting of two chambers, the National Council and the Council of States, serving as the Swiss parliament.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Schweizer Regierung">
      <data key="d0">Schweizer Regierung</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Swiss government known as the Federal Council, designed as a collegiate body without a single head of state, emphasizing shared leadership.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Staatliche Ebenen">
      <data key="d0">Staatliche Ebenen</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The three political levels of Switzerland: Municipality, Canton, and Federation, where districts are part of state administrative structures without independent political authority.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bund">
      <data key="d0">Bund</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The federation encompassing all Swiss cantons, representing the highest political level in Switzerland and possessing overall legislative competence.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Parlament">
      <data key="d0">Parlament</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The legislative body in Switzerland, referred to as the Federal Assembly, comprised of the National Council and the Council of States, with each serving specific roles in governance.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="schweizerische Bundesverfassung">
      <data key="d0">schweizerische Bundesverfassung</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Swiss Federal Constitution, which was established in 1848, serves as the fundamental legal document for Switzerland, subject to continuous revision and major renewals in 1874 and 1999.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kantone">
      <data key="d0">Kantone</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The cantons of Switzerland, which are semi-autonomous regions playing a crucial role in the federal system and enjoying significant powers of self-government.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bezirke">
      <data key="d0">Bezirke</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Districts within cantons that serve administrative functions but do not possess political autonomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Volksvertretung">
      <data key="d0">Volksvertretung</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The concept of popular representation within the Swiss political system, exemplified by the National Council as the voice of the citizenry.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Interpellation">
      <data key="d0">Interpellation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A parliamentary procedure in Switzerland that allows legislators to question government actions and seek clarification.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Motion">
      <data key="d0">Motion</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A proposal submitted in parliament to express the opinion or request actions from the government.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Postulat">
      <data key="d0">Postulat</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A formal request in the Swiss parliament that urges the government to investigate a particular issue or situation.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Subsidiarität">
      <data key="d0">Subsidiarität</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The principle of subsidiarity in Swiss governance, emphasizing that decisions should be taken at the most local level possible unless they require higher authority.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urnengang">
      <data key="d0">Urnengang</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of voting in Switzerland, particularly in relation to direct democracy mechanisms.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Schweizer Demokratie">
      <data key="d0">Schweizer Demokratie</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Swiss democracy is fundamentally representative, involving citizens directly in legislative processes and reflecting a complex relationship between parliamentary and direct democratic elements since its total revision in 1874.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bundesverfassung">
      <data key="d0">Bundesverfassung</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Bundesverfassung is the Federal Constitution of Switzerland, outlining the country's fundamental laws, including the principles of federalism and the distribution of powers between the canton and the federal level.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bundesverfassung is the Federal Constitution of Switzerland, which is subject to changes that require a popular vote and is primarily determined by the parliament.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Volksinitiative">
      <data key="d0">Volksinitiative</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Volksinitiative is a method allowing a certain number of Swiss citizens (100,000) to propose changes to the federal constitution by gathering signatures for a popular vote.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedural method in Switzerland that allows citizens to propose changes to the constitution or legislation, requiring a certain number of signatures for consideration.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-006</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Referendumsrecht">
      <data key="d0">Referendumsrecht</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Referendumsrecht is the right of the populace to challenge parliamentary decisions through a subsequent referendum, where they can either confirm or annul those decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Wahlrecht">
      <data key="d0">Wahlrecht</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Wahlrecht is the voting right in Switzerland granting all Swiss citizens aged 18 and above, including those residing abroad, the ability to vote in elections unless disqualified for specific reasons.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stimmrecht">
      <data key="d0">Stimmrecht</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Stimmrecht is the right of eligible voters to vote on local, cantonal, and national proposals, allowing direct participation in the legislative process.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kantonale Rechte">
      <data key="d0">Kantonale Rechte</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The kantonale Rechte refers to the rights of the citizens at the cantonal level in Switzerland, which often surpass those at the federal level, allowing for greater participation in governance.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gesetzesinitiative">
      <data key="d0">Gesetzesinitiative</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Gesetzesinitiative is a method that enables citizens to propose new laws or amendments to existing ones, requiring fewer signatures than constitutional changes, thereby facilitating local governance.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Finanzreferendum">
      <data key="d0">Finanzreferendum</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Finanzreferendum is a type of referendum concerning the approval of specific financial expenditures, which can be either mandatory or optional based on cantonal regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of referendum related to financial matters in Swiss cantons, which may be optional or mandatory depending on expenditure thresholds defined by cantonal constitutions.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-006</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stimmberechtigte">
      <data key="d0">Stimmberechtigte</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Stimmberechtigte refers to eligible voters in Switzerland who meet the criteria to participate in elections and referendums.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vizepräsident">
      <data key="d0">Vizepräsident</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Vizepräsident is a member of the Bundesrat elected to serve as the Vice President of the Swiss Federal Council for a one-year term, assisting the Bundespräsident in ceremonial duties.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-002</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bundeskanzlei">
      <data key="d0">Bundeskanzlei</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Bundeskanzlei is the Federal Chancellery of Switzerland, responsible for the central coordination and communication between the government, administration, parliament, and the public. It is led by the Bundeskanzler.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-002</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bundeskanzler">
      <data key="d0">Bundeskanzler</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Bundeskanzler is the head of the Bundeskanzlei, attending all meetings of the Bundesrat without voting rights and overseeing the preparation of council meetings.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-002</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Guy Parmelin">
      <data key="d0">Guy Parmelin</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Guy Parmelin is a member of the Bundesrat from the canton of Vaud and serves as the Bundespräsident for 2026.&lt;SEP&gt;Guy Parmelin is a member of the Swiss Federal Council serving as the Federal President in 2026, affiliated with the Swiss People's Party (SVP) from the Waadt (VD) canton, with a background as a master farmer and winemaker.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-94e3e6258ab98fce781a5c362a19d85b-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784834653</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ignazio Cassis">
      <data key="d0">Ignazio Cassis</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Ignazio Cassis is a member of the Bundesrat from the canton of Ticino and serves as the Vizepräsident for 2026.&lt;SEP&gt;Ignazio Cassis holds the position of Vice President in 2026, representing the FDP from the Tessin (TI) canton. He has a medical background, having served as the former cantonal physician.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-94e3e6258ab98fce781a5c362a19d85b-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784834653</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Karin Keller-Sutter">
      <data key="d0">Karin Keller-Sutter</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Karin Keller-Sutter is a member of the Bundesrat from the canton of St. Gallen.&lt;SEP&gt;Karin Keller-Sutter is a member of the Swiss Federal Council affiliated with the FDP, serving from the St. Gallen (SG) canton. She has a background as a translator and former government councilor of St. Gallen.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-94e3e6258ab98fce781a5c362a19d85b-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784834653</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Albert Rösti">
      <data key="d0">Albert Rösti</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Albert Rösti is a member of the Bundesrat from the canton of Bern.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-002</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Elisabeth Baume-Schneider">
      <data key="d0">Elisabeth Baume-Schneider</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Elisabeth Baume-Schneider is a member of the Bundesrat from the canton of Jura.&lt;SEP&gt;Elisabeth Baume-Schneider serves as a member of the Swiss Federal Council, affiliated with the Social Democratic Party (SP) from Jura (JU) canton, with a professional background in social work.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-94e3e6258ab98fce781a5c362a19d85b-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784834653</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Beat Jans">
      <data key="d0">Beat Jans</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Beat Jans is a member of the Bundesrat from the canton of Basel-Stadt.&lt;SEP&gt;Beat Jans is a member of the Swiss Federal Council and represents the Social Democratic Party (SP) from the Basel-Stadt (BS) canton, with expertise in environmental science.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-94e3e6258ab98fce781a5c362a19d85b-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784834653</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Martin Pfister">
      <data key="d0">Martin Pfister</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Martin Pfister is a member of the Bundesrat from the canton of Zug.&lt;SEP&gt;Martin Pfister is a member of the Swiss Federal Council representing the party Die Mitte from the Zug (ZG) canton, recognized as a Swiss politician.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-94e3e6258ab98fce781a5c362a19d85b-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784834653</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Regierungsrat">
      <data key="d0">Regierungsrat</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Regierungsrat is the executive body in most German-speaking cantons of Switzerland, functioning similarly to the Bundesrat at the cantonal level.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-002</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Staatsrat">
      <data key="d0">Staatsrat</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Staatsrat is the executive body in predominantly French-speaking cantons and the canton of Ticino, analogous to the Bundesrat.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-002</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Standeskommission">
      <data key="d0">Standeskommission</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Standeskommission is the executive body in the canton of Appenzell Innerrhoden, serving a similar function as the Bundesrat.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-002</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Primus inter Pares">
      <data key="d0">Primus inter Pares</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Primus inter Pares is a title given to a member of the cantonal executive who leads the government meetings for one year without being termed a governmental leader.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-002</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gericht">
      <data key="d0">Gericht</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Gericht refers to various types of courts within the Swiss legal system, including first-instance courts, second-instance courts, and specialized courts for different legal matters.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Obergericht">
      <data key="d0">Obergericht</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Obergericht is the term for a second-instance court in many Swiss cantons, responsible for appeals from lower courts.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Appellationsgericht">
      <data key="d0">Appellationsgericht</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Appellationsgericht is a type of appellate court that handles civil law disputes, existing in some cantons such as Basel-Stadt and Ticino.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cour de justice">
      <data key="d0">Cour de justice</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Cour de justice is a type of court specific to the canton of Geneva, responsible for legal adjudications.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Zwangsmassnahmengerichte">
      <data key="d0">Zwangsmassnahmengerichte</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Zwangsmassnahmengerichte are courts responsible for the issuance of coercive measures related to investigations and security detention in various cantons.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Verwaltungsgericht">
      <data key="d0">Verwaltungsgericht</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Verwaltungsgericht refers to administrative courts that manage public law disputes in larger cantons, distinct from other judicial entities.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Spezialverwaltungsgerichte">
      <data key="d0">Spezialverwaltungsgerichte</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Spezialverwaltungsgerichte are specialized administrative courts for unique legal matters, such as building or tax appeals.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Jugendgerichte">
      <data key="d0">Jugendgerichte</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Jugendgerichte are specialized courts dealing with youth-related legal issues, commonly found across various cantons.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Strafgericht">
      <data key="d0">Strafgericht</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Strafgericht specifically refers to courts that address criminal cases in certain cantons, handling serious offenses by replacing district or higher courts.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kassationsgericht">
      <data key="d0">Kassationsgericht</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Kassationsgericht is a court of cassation that reviewed appeals before its elimination in 2011, formally addressing procedural legal matters.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Verfassungsgericht">
      <data key="d0">Verfassungsgericht</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Verfassungsgericht pertains to constitutional courts that some cantons, like Basel-Stadt, Geneva, and Jura, have established to oversee constitutional matters.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="direkte Demokratie">
      <data key="d0">direkte Demokratie</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Direkte Demokratie denotes a form of democratic governance where citizens can directly participate in decision-making processes, complemented by representative elements.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="repräsentative Demokratie">
      <data key="d0">repräsentative Demokratie</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Repräsentative Demokratie is a system of governance where elected representatives make decisions on behalf of the citizens, central to the functioning of the Swiss state.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kanton Zürich">
      <data key="d0">Kanton Zürich</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A geographic region in Switzerland known for its unique political instruments such as the Einzelinitiative, which allows individual initiatives regarding constitutional or legal changes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-006</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kanton Freiburg">
      <data key="d0">Kanton Freiburg</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A canton in western Switzerland, known for its bilingual population and varied cultural heritage.&lt;SEP&gt;A canton in Switzerland that historically maintained a representative democratic government longer than others, until it was replaced by direct-democratic institutions in 1918.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-003</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kanton Schaffhausen">
      <data key="d0">Kanton Schaffhausen</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A canton in northern Switzerland, known for the Rhine Falls and its historic town.&lt;SEP&gt;A canton in Switzerland that recognizes the Volksmotion, which allows a specified number of people to submit a motion to the cantonal parliament.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-003</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kanton Solothurn">
      <data key="d0">Kanton Solothurn</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A canton in the northwest of Switzerland, recognized for its historical architecture and natural beauty.&lt;SEP&gt;A canton in Switzerland that has adopted the Volksmotion, enabling a certain number of individuals to submit motions to the cantonal parliament.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-003</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kanton Appenzell Innerrhoden">
      <data key="d0">Kanton Appenzell Innerrhoden</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A canton in Switzerland, known for its traditional rural culture and direct democracy practices.&lt;SEP&gt;A Swiss canton that recognizes the right of the Einzelinitiative, requiring personal initiatives to be presented to the Landsgemeinde.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-003</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kanton Glarus">
      <data key="d0">Kanton Glarus</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A Swiss canton that also requires personal initiatives to be presented to the Landsgemeinde under the Einzelinitiative system.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-006</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kanton Appenzell Ausserrhoden">
      <data key="d0">Kanton Appenzell Ausserrhoden</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A canton in Switzerland that features the Volksdiskussion, allowing every resident to express their opinion on legislative proposals.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-006</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kanton Bern">
      <data key="d0">Kanton Bern</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A canton in Switzerland that recognizes the constructive referendum, enabling citizens to introduce alternatives to parliamentary proposals.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-003</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kanton Nidwalden">
      <data key="d0">Kanton Nidwalden</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A canton in Switzerland that has implemented the constructive referendum since 1996, similar to Kanton Bern.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-006</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kanton Basel-Landschaft">
      <data key="d0">Kanton Basel-Landschaft</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A Swiss canton that employs a mixed approach to referendums, allowing certain laws passed by a majority in the cantonal parliament to undergo optional referendum.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-006</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kantonsrat">
      <data key="d0">Kantonsrat</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The cantonal parliament in Switzerland where members can propose various legislative initiatives and motions.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-006</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Volksabstimmung">
      <data key="d0">Volksabstimmung</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A form of direct democratic decision-making in Switzerland where citizens vote on specific legislative proposals or changes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-006</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Einzelinitiative">
      <data key="d0">Einzelinitiative</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedural method in Switzerland allowing individuals to propose amendments to the constitution or laws, requiring the support of other members.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-67a86848d7820be81a8cc7885716f2f2-chunk-006</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1784799658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Volksmotion">
